A||||7||GT Bastard||clutch???? Solid hub or spring hub?||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||04-13-2000||10:34 PM||||Anybody know the difference between solid hub and spring hub clutch discs?  How does it affect driveability??? thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-14-2000||12:49 AM||||?||||reg||1
Z||000002||2FAST4U||04-14-2000||12:50 AM||||A sprung hub is just as it says, it has springs in the hub. These springs are there to absorb some of the shock to the driveline when you release the clutch.<BR>An unsprung hub is just that, a solid hub.<BR>If you are driving your car on the street always use a sprung hub.<BR>The solid hub clutch was designed for drag racing to shock the rear tires during launch.<BR>There is absolutly no reason or advantage to using a solid hub on a street car and doing so is very hard on the CV Joints, axles, transaxle, and the clutch itself.<BR>Hope this helps.<P>Joe<BR>2fast4u||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||04-14-2000||12:52 AM||||I haven't seen a solid hub clutch in a long time. The reason they have springs is so that they take up the shock of the tranny shifting. A solid hub also will chatter and not last as long.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phoenix||04-14-2000||08:22 AM||||Clutch disks designed for street with a "solid" hub are usually made with a hard rubber center section connecting the disk to the splined shaft.  The Rubber takes up the shock the same way the springs do in a spring disk. The rubber center solid disk works fine until they get old.  The rubber will start to crack and break up and little pieces can get caught between the disk/pressure plate and flywheel making it almost impossible to disengage the clutch. This was a common problem with mid/late 70's and early 80's Porsche 911's.  Stick with a spring disk.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||04-14-2000||08:45 AM||||the only people that use a solid vulcan rubber center is Porsche and Lancia. I've killed a RAM, a Centerforce DF, and an all stock clutch in the 18 months i've owned my car.. the solid hub will take a bit to get used to but it's not as bad as every one says... alot like those that say lowering springs make the car undrivable cause its too stiff or bouncy... When done correctly its not a problem... I've had my tranny out to put on new cradle and motor mounts and inspected the flywheel and pressure plate twice since the install of my ACT unit.. there was noticable wear on my flywheel and pressure plate (6000 miles) but no really bad wear on the disc. I'm still railing on the car, the tranny, axles are fine I have 8000 on it now and no problems...  well i take that back I have difficulty getting into reverse... thats why I'm looking for a 4:10 tranny... but thats not clutch related, thats the only problem... the solid hub is the only clutch that will NOT slip under  under ANY conditions I've tossed at it. 310lbft of torque or stock at a WEAK 200lbft...  I'll get todd (BlackGT) to give his $.02... ||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackGT||04-14-2000||09:33 AM||||Well let me through in my $.02<BR>Yes a solid hub clutch will chatter for a little<BR>while when you first install it but then you also need to relearn how to drive your car<BR>when you put one of these puppies in. 1FAST2M6 convinced me to do this and I<BR>could kiss that guy! This car launches with<BR>authority.....will smoke the tires when I feel<BR>like and most of all IT DON`T SLIP!!! Yes<BR>It`s a little tricky to drive at first but you all<BR>remember the first time you drove your Fieros the gears were so close together. <BR>Well this will be a new experience for ya as well........pulls to redline and you feel it pulling in any gear. I drive my car every day<BR>and I`m kinda hard at times put about 3k miles on since intalling this clutch and you ask do I like it .......YA DAMN SKIPPY!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||BlackGT||04-14-2000||09:36 AM||||One more thing........in order to prevent<BR>the engine from rocking back and forth during lauches when my pockets allow <BR>I`m replacing all the cradle bushings!!||||reg||1
